Z||000000||goldpan||07-30-99||09:53 AM||||My temp gage is pegged full left on cold start and moves to the 1st line when it should be at normal temp.  Is this a gage  or sending unit problem???
Z||000001||Andy||08-02-99||02:31 PM||||Goldpan,try reading "wich temp sensor is wich" on 7-20,"temp gauge fix" on 7-27 and "temperature gauge freaking out" on 8-2 ,maybe there can help you, too.It sure been helping me to narrow things down.Thanks guys for all the help
Z||000002||Ozzy||08-08-99||02:31 AM||||I will describe the process I experienced with my temp guage problems and what I did to fix it.<p>My guage was seemingly showing near overheating on the day that I took delivery of the car. After determining that the car was not running that hot I began to investigate. First I purchased what I thought was a new temp sending unit. It was the one on the thermostat housing. (I have the 2.5L iron duke engine in an '88 coupe) This is NOT the temp sending unit. It is the cooling fan switch. It may have other functions but to my knowledge this is it's main one. Obviously this was not the problem but the unit I got was cheap so what the hell...now it's new. I then bought the actual temp sending unit (located on the rear left side of the head). I figured it was the guage or this sender to be the problem so...after installing the new sender, no change! So I start to pull the dash apart to access the guage. Now I had noticed all the while that the guage had kind of a loose eratic movement. Seemed like the mechanism of the guage itself was shot. As I began to dissasemble that bastard dash I noticed something. The needle on the guage center spindle seemed to be off center. As I managed to remove the clear plastic gauge panel I could see what the problem was. The needle was loose and rotating freely on the center pin. I assume this is from pinning on startup and such. Don't know why they send battery voltage direct thru this guage on ignition but...I put a small dab of super glue on the needle being VERY carefull to make sure the needle was aligned in position and VOILA! BINGO! and all that. The guage now works perfectly. Now the sending unit may have been faulty too but I have not reinstalled the old one to verify. Hope something here helps. I've read on other sites that the "pinning"of the temp guage is common and this could certainly cause anybodys temp guage to do what mine did.<p>Ozzy
